Setup. Let $\mathbb{N}_0=\{0,1,2,\dots\}$ and let $\ell_\infty(\mathbb{N}_0)$ denote the space of bounded scalar sequences $x=(\xi_0,\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)$. A linear subspace $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)\subseteq\ell_\infty(\mathbb{N}_0)$ equipped with a quasi-norm $\|\cdot\|_{\mathfrak{z}}$ is called a quasi-Banach shift-monotone sequence ideal if:
  (i) $(\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0),\|\cdot\|_{\mathfrak{z}})$ is complete;
  (ii) it contains the canonical unit sequences;
  (iii) (monotonicity) whenever $x\in\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ and $y\in\ell_\infty(\mathbb{N}_0)$ satisfy $|\eta_k|\le|\xi_k|$ for all $k$, then $y\in\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ with $\|y\|_{\mathfrak{z}}\le\|x\|_{\mathfrak{z}}$;
  (iv) (shift-invariance/monotonicity under shifts) the backward shift $S_-:(\xi_0,\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)\mapsto(\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)$ and the forward shift $S_+:(\xi_0,\xi_1,\dots)\mapsto(0,\xi_0,\xi_1,\dots)$ both map $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ into itself continuously.

Consider the bounded linear operator
$$T:=I-2S_-:\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)\longrightarrow\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0),\qquad T(\xi_0,\xi_1,\xi_2,\dots)=(\xi_0-2\xi_1,\;\xi_1-2\xi_2,\;\xi_2-2\xi_3,\dots),$$
and let $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z}):=T(\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0))$ denote its range. The following mutually exclusive cases for this range are a priori possible:
  Case 1. $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})$ is closed and equal to $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ (codimension $0$);
  Case 2. $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})$ is not closed in $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$;
  Case 3. $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})$ is closed in $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ with finite positive codimension (i.e. $\dim\bigl(\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)/M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})\bigr)\in\{1,2,3,\dots\}$);
  Case 4. $M(I-2S_-\,|\,\mathfrak{z})$ is closed with infinite codimension.

Problem. Determine whether Case 3 can actually occur. Equivalently, decide whether there exists a quasi-Banach shift-monotone sequence ideal $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ such that the range of $I-2S_-$ on $\mathfrak{z}(\mathbb{N}_0)$ is closed and has finite codimension strictly between $1$ and $\infty$, or prove that no such ideal exists.

